================================================================================
Where the facts come from
================================================================================

This tool makes a small number of claims about how a server behaves, and each was
established from a primary source rather than from recollection. Line numbers were
read on 2026-08-25 from the default branch of each repository unless a tag is named.

  Commands typed into a process that has no terminal
    The whole chain was read, because the design depends on it. Paper replaces
    vanilla's console reader: org.bukkit.craftbukkit.Main declares useConsole
    (Main.java:16), and the patch to DedicatedServer starts PaperConsole in place
    of vanilla's own stdin loop, which the same patch comments out
    (DedicatedServer.java.patch:42-56, thread started at :117). PaperConsole
    extends Minecrell's SimpleTerminalConsole and sends each line to
    handleConsoleInput (PaperConsole.java:13,45-47). SimpleTerminalConsole asks
    JLine for a system terminal and, when there is none, reads System.in line by
    line instead (SimpleTerminalConsole.java:137-148,177-184, at tag 1.3.0). JLine
    only attempts a system terminal when stdin is one (TerminalBuilder.java:472-477
    at tag jline-3.27.1), which a pipe is not, and TerminalConsoleAppender turns the
    resulting failure into a single warning about advanced terminal features
    (TerminalConsoleAppender.java:230-246). handleConsoleInput puts the line on a
    concurrent queue drained by the tick thread (DedicatedServer.java.patch:290-306),
    and Folia drains the same queue on its global region tick
    (0001-Region-Threading-Base.patch:1815). So a piped command arrives, and one
    sent before the server is ready is queued rather than dropped.

  The line that says a plugin is enabling
    Read from PaperMC/Paper PaperPluginInstanceManager.java:186-190: the message is
    "Enabling " followed by the plugin's display name, logged through the plugin's
    own logger, with a trailing asterisk when the plugin declares no api-version.
    That is what the scenario key `enabled:` waits for.

  The line that says a server is ready
    Not sourceable from Paper's repository: it belongs to vanilla code, which Paper
    does not hold. It was taken from real boots of Paper 1.21.4 and Purpur 1.21.4 on
    2026-08-25 and is matched on its timing part alone, which is why `ready:` exists
    as a scenario key. Nothing in this tool claims a source it does not have.

  Matching the initial heap to the maximum
    From PaperMC/docs (BSD-2-Clause), paper/admin/how-to/aikars-flags.md. mcstage
    adds no other tuning flag, because a harness has no business having opinions
    about somebody else's heap.

  Where builds come from
    PaperMC's fill API v3, verified by request on 2026-08-25: /v3/projects answers a
    list of projects each carrying its versions grouped by family, newest first, and
    a build carries downloads["server:default"] with a name, a size, a sha256 and a
    URL under fill-data addressed by that same digest. Purpur's API v2, verified the
    same day: /v2/purpur lists versions oldest first, /v2/purpur/{version} carries
    builds.latest and builds.all, and a build carries a result and an md5. Both are
    asked with a User-Agent naming this tool, as such APIs ask. Nothing about a
    scenario, an instance or a plugin is ever sent anywhere.

  The EULA
    mcstage writes eula=true only when --accept-eula is passed, and never uses the
    JVM property that bypasses the file. The agreement is the user's to make.

